<!-- Detail Card Field -->
<div
  class="st-DetailCard-field"
  style="padding: 10px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e8f0;"
>
  {{#if title}}
    <p
      class="st-DetailCard-label"
      style="Margin:0 0 4px 0;font-size:11px;font-family:'Inter', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;line-height:16px;color:#64748b;font-weight:600;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.5px;"
    >{{title}}</p>
  {{/if}}
  {{!--
    THE ESCAPING CONTRACT FOR THIS PARTIAL.

    `text` is RAW HTML and is rendered unescaped. Only pass it a value that
    was produced by Markdown.convertToHTML (which escapes the raw-HTML tokens
    a user typed) or by
    OneUptimeDate.getDateAsFormattedHTMLInMultipleTimezones (which emits
    <br/> of its own).

    `plainText` is escaped by Handlebars at render time. Everything else
    belongs here: alert titles, monitor names, series labels, severity and
    state names, user names. Those are composed from Kubernetes resource
    attributes and from free text a project member typed, and a value
    containing markup in `text` puts an attacker-authored <a href> into an
    email the recipient trusts.

    The same rule is already written down for the rollup email in
    Common/Server/Utils/EmailRollup/EmailRollupRenderer.ts - "a raw value in a
    triple stache is an XSS hole in somebody's mail client". This partial is
    where that rule is enforced for everyone else.

    The two branches are deliberately separate rather than one branch with a
    flag: ~330 existing call sites pass HTML through `text` and must stay
    byte-identical, and the HTML/plain split is not expressible per variable
    name (incidentDescription is Markdown-HTML in one sender and raw in
    another).

    `blockText` is the third slot, and carries the SAME escaping contract as
    `text` — raw HTML, Markdown.convertToHTML output only. It exists because
    `text` renders inside a <p>, and Markdown.convertToHTML emits BLOCK
    elements: the breaching-samples <table> in an alert root cause, and the
    <ul> in any description written as a list. A <table> inside a <p> is
    invalid nesting, and every mail client resolves it by auto-closing the
    paragraph early — so the table escaped the styled .st-DetailCard-value
    box and landed unstyled against the card edge. This branch is a <div>
    carrying identical typography, so block content nests legally and still
    looks like every other field.

    Use `blockText` for anything that came out of Markdown.convertToHTML;
    `text` remains correct for inline HTML such as
    OneUptimeDate.getDateAsFormattedHTMLInMultipleTimezones.
  --}}
  {{#if blockText}}
    <div
      class="st-DetailCard-value"
      style="Margin:0;font-size:15px;font-family:'Inter', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;line-height:24px;color:#1e293b;font-weight:500;"
    >{{{blockText}}}</div>
  {{/if}}
  {{#if text}}
    <p
      class="st-DetailCard-value"
      style="Margin:0;font-size:15px;font-family:'Inter', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;line-height:24px;color:#1e293b;font-weight:500;"
    >{{{text}}}</p>
  {{/if}}
  {{#if plainText}}
    <p
      class="st-DetailCard-value"
      style="Margin:0;font-size:15px;font-family:'Inter', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;line-height:24px;color:#1e293b;font-weight:500;"
    >{{plainText}}</p>
  {{/if}}
</div>